# Line Monitoring & Data Management — Run Manual

This is the complete guide to running every piece of this project: what
each folder is, what to install, exact commands, and how to troubleshoot.

## What's in this project

```
project/
├── backend/              FastAPI + SQL Server API — runs on the Server PC
├── frontend/              React dashboard (real, API-connected) — Server PC
├── frontend-static/       HTML/CSS mockup (no backend needed) — for design review
├── line-client/            Agent that runs on each of the ~60 line PCs
├── excel-import-tool/     One-time/occasional tool to bulk-load lines from Excel
├── schema.sql (in backend/)  Raw SQL Server schema script
```

Two kinds of machines:

- **1 Server PC** — database + backend + website. Needs a fixed LAN IP.
- **~60 Line PCs** — one `line-client` agent per production line.

---

## Part A — Quick Local Test (one PC, no SQL Server needed)

Do this first, on your own laptop/dev PC, before touching the real factory
network. It uses SQLite instead of SQL Server so there's nothing to install
except Python and Node.

### Prerequisites

- Python 3.11+ (`python --version`)
- Node.js 18+ (`node --version`) — only needed for the React frontend
- (Optional) `pip install pytest` if you want to run the test suites

### 1. Backend

```
cd backend
pip install -r requirements.txt
copy .env.example .env
```

Open `.env` and confirm this line is present (it is, by default):
```
DATABASE_URL=sqlite:///./dev.db
```

Create the schema and start the server:
```
python -m alembic upgrade head
uvicorn app.main:app --reload --port 8000
```

Check it's alive — open a second terminal:
```
curl http://localhost:8000/health
```
Expect: `{"status":"ok"}`

### 2. Load some test lines (no Excel needed for this quick test)

```
cd backend
python scripts/generate_admin_token.py
```
Copy the printed token. Then, using any HTTP client (or `curl`):
```
curl -X POST http://localhost:8000/api/v1/lines ^
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" ^
  -H "Authorization: Bearer PASTE_TOKEN_HERE" ^
  -d "{\"line_number\":\"LINE01\",\"ip_address\":\"192.168.1.101\",\"total_dl\":19,\"total_ft\":8}"
```
(`^` is the line-continuation character in Windows `cmd`; use `\` in PowerShell/bash.)

```
curl http://localhost:8000/api/v1/lines
```
You should see `LINE01` in the response.

### 3. Frontend (React)

In a new terminal:
```
cd frontend
npm install
npm run dev
```
Open the printed URL (usually `http://localhost:5173`). The dashboard
should show `LINE01`. The dev server proxies `/api` and `/ws` to
`localhost:8000` automatically (see `vite.config.ts`).

### 4. Static HTML/CSS mockup (no backend needed at all)

```
cd frontend-static
```
Just double-click `index.html`, or serve the folder with any static file
server. This is mock data only — useful for design review, not for real
monitoring.

### 5. Line client agent (simulate one line PC)

```
cd line-client
pip install -r requirements.txt
```
Create `.env`:
```
LINE_NUMBER=LINE01
SERVER_URL=http://localhost:8000
CLIENT_SECRET=change-me-client-secret
BASE_DIR=C:\InLine_Pro
```
(`CLIENT_SECRET` must match the backend's `.env` — both default to
`change-me-client-secret` out of the box, so they already match for this
local test.)

If you don't have a real `C:\InLine_Pro\Data.ini` / `Log\` folder yet, point
`BASE_DIR` at the test fixtures instead so you can see it work end-to-end:
```
BASE_DIR=line-client\tests\fixtures
```
Then run:
```
python main.py
```
Watch the backend terminal / the dashboard — `LINE01` should flip to
`ONLINE` within one poll cycle (60 seconds by default;
`POLL_INTERVAL_SECONDS` in `.env` to change it).

### 6. Run the automated tests

```
cd backend
python -m pytest tests/ -v
```
```
cd line-client
python -m pytest tests/ -v
```
Both should report all tests passing before you move to Part B.

---

## Part B — Full Production Rollout

Once Part A works on your own machine, repeat the real version across the
factory network.

### Step 1 — Server PC: Database

1. Install SQL Server 2014 and SQL Server Management Studio (SSMS), or
   confirm they're already installed.
2. Open `backend/schema.sql` in SSMS and run it (F5). This creates the
   `LineMonitoring` database and all four tables.
3. In SSMS's Object Explorer, confirm `lines`, `line_units`,
   `unit_current_state`, and `monitoring_events` all exist under
   `LineMonitoring > Tables`.

### Step 2 — Server PC: Backend

1. Install Python 3.11+ and the **ODBC Driver 17 for SQL Server**
   (download from Microsoft if not already present).
2. Copy the `backend/` folder onto the Server PC.
3. `cd backend && pip install -r requirements.txt`
4. `copy .env.example .env`, then edit `.env`:
   ```
   DATABASE_URL=mssql+pyodbc://USER:PASSWORD@localhost:1433/LineMonitoring?driver=ODBC+Driver+17+for+SQL+Server
   CLIENT_SECRET=<pick a real secret — every line PC will need this exact value>
   JWT_SECRET=<pick a different secret>
   ```
5. **Import your Excel file now** (Step 3 below) so the dashboard has data
   the first time it loads.
6. Start the backend so it's reachable from other PCs on the LAN:
   ```
   uvicorn app.main:app --host 0.0.0.0 --port 8000
   ```
7. From a *different* PC on the LAN:
   ```
   curl http://<server-ip>:8000/health
   ```
   should return `{"status":"ok"}`.
8. In Windows Firewall on the Server PC, add an inbound rule allowing TCP
   port 8000 (and whatever port you serve the website on) from the line-PC
   subnet.

### Step 3 — Server PC: Import Your Excel File

```
cd excel-import-tool
pip install -r requirements.txt
copy .env.example .env
```
Edit `.env` — same `DATABASE_URL` as the backend's.

```
python import_lines_from_excel.py path\to\your\lines.xlsx --dry-run
python import_lines_from_excel.py path\to\your\lines.xlsx
```
Accepted column headers (case-insensitive): `Line No`/`Line Number`,
`IP Address`/`IP`, `Total DL`/`DL`, `Total FT`/`FT`. Re-run any time your
line list changes — it updates existing lines and adds new ones, never
deletes.

### Step 4 — Server PC: Website

**React app (recommended — actually connects to live data):**
```
cd frontend
npm install
npm run build
```
Serve the resulting `dist/` folder with IIS, nginx, or any static file
server, configured to proxy `/api` and `/ws` requests through to
`http://localhost:8000`.

**Or the static HTML/CSS mockup** (`frontend-static/`) — just for visual
review; it has no live data.

### Step 5 — Each Line PC: Client Agent

Repeat for all ~60 machines. The code is identical everywhere — only two
`.env` values change per PC.

1. Install Python 3.11+.
2. Copy the `line-client/` folder onto the line PC.
3. `cd line-client && pip install -r requirements.txt`
4. Create `.env`:
   ```
   LINE_NUMBER=LINE01
   SERVER_URL=http://192.168.1.10:8000
   CLIENT_SECRET=<same value as the backend's CLIENT_SECRET>
   ```
   `LINE_NUMBER` must exactly match what you imported from Excel for this
   PC. `BASE_DIR` defaults to `C:\InLine_Pro`, which should already exist
   from the line's existing monitoring software — no need to set it unless
   your paths differ.
5. Test it manually first: `python main.py`, confirm no errors, then
   `Ctrl+C` to stop.
6. Set it to run automatically:
   - **Simple:** Task Scheduler → New Task → Trigger: "At startup" →
     Action: run `python.exe` with argument `main.py`, "Start in" set to
     the `line-client` folder → check "Run whether user is logged on or
     not".
   - **More robust:** package as a single `.exe` with PyInstaller and point
     Task Scheduler at that instead of raw Python (this is the intended
     Phase 9 packaging step — not yet built).

### Step 6 — Verify

1. Open the dashboard on the Server PC (or any PC on the LAN).
2. Within one poll interval, each connected line should show as `ONLINE`
   with DL/FT counts matching `Data.ini`.
3. Stop one line PC's agent and confirm its card goes `STALE` then
   `OFFLINE` after the configured timeouts.
4. Change a line's IP/DL/FT count in your Excel file, re-run the import
   tool, and confirm the change shows up (update, not a duplicate).

---

## Day-to-Day Operations

**Restart the backend** (after a config change):
```
cd backend
# stop the running uvicorn process (Ctrl+C), then:
uvicorn app.main:app --host 0.0.0.0 --port 8000
```

**Add/update lines** whenever your Excel sheet changes:
```
cd excel-import-tool
python import_lines_from_excel.py path\to\lines.xlsx
```

**Check a line PC's agent is running:** look for the `python main.py`
process, or check Task Scheduler's task history. Logs print to the console
(or redirect to a file: `python main.py >> agent.log 2>&1`).

**Run the tests after making any code change:**
```
cd backend && python -m pytest tests/ -v
cd line-client && python -m pytest tests/ -v
```

---

## Troubleshooting

| Symptom | Likely cause | Fix |
|---|---|---|
| `curl http://localhost:8000/health` fails | Backend not running, or wrong port | Check the `uvicorn` terminal for errors; confirm the port matches |
| Line PC's dashboard card never goes `ONLINE` | Wrong `CLIENT_SECRET`, or `LINE_NUMBER` doesn't match a line already in the database | Check `line-client`'s console output for a `401` or `404`; confirm the line was imported via Excel first |
| `401 Unauthorized` on `/api/v1/line/status` | `CLIENT_SECRET` mismatch between backend `.env` and line-client `.env` | Make them identical, restart both |
| `404 Not Found` (`LINE_NOT_FOUND`) on ingestion | The line PC's `LINE_NUMBER` isn't in the `lines` table yet | Import it via `excel-import-tool` first |
| Excel import script errors on column names | Header text doesn't match any accepted alias | Rename the column, or add the exact header text you're using to `COLUMN_ALIASES` in `import_lines_from_excel.py` |
| `pyodbc` install fails | ODBC Driver 17 for SQL Server not installed on that machine | Install it from Microsoft's site, then retry `pip install` |
| Dashboard shows a line as `STALE`/`OFFLINE` even though it's running | `ScanSendPoint` hasn't appeared in the log within the 45-minute window, or the log file/date doesn't match `find_todays_log`'s expected filename pattern | Confirm `C:\InLine_Pro\Log\{YYYYMMDD}_New.Log` exists for today's date and contains recent `[Front]`/`[Rear]ScanSendPoint` lines |
| Two browsers show different data | One is pointed at the static mockup (`frontend-static/`), the other at the real React app | Use the React app (`frontend/`) for anything that needs live data |

